    title: 'Irvine Dog Bite Lawyer',
    eyebrow: 'Dog bites in Irvine, California',
    description: 'If a dog bit you in Irvine, California law generally makes the owner responsible for a bite in a public place or while lawfully on private property, even if the dog never bit anyone before. Kyle Scott Law represents people bitten or knocked down by dogs in Irvine’s parks, neighborhoods, and apartment communities.',
    metaDescription: 'Irvine dog bite lawyer for bites in parks, apartment courtyards and near off-leash dog parks, including workers bitten on the job. Free consultation.',
    icon: 'dog',
    introTitle: 'An Irvine dog bite claim turns on where it happened and who pays.',
    intro: [
      'A dog’s owner is generally liable for a bite in a public place or while the injured person was lawfully on private property, including the owner’s own home, regardless of the dog’s history or the owner’s knowledge of it (Civil Code § 3342). Get medical care first, even for a bite that looks small: dog bites can become infected, and the medical record is also the first record of the injury. The legal questions come next: who owned the dog, where the bite happened, and which insurance covers the owner. Scarring and nerve damage may not be clear for weeks, so an early settlement can leave out future care.',
      'Kyle Scott has practiced law since 1991 and has run his own firm since 2003. He identifies the owner and any landlord or property manager involved, obtains the Irvine Animal Services bite report, and handles the insurance claim and, if needed, the lawsuit. If you are looking for a dog bite attorney in Irvine, your first conversation is with Kyle Scott himself.',
    ],
    keyPoints: [
      { title: 'Parks, trails, and sidewalks', body: 'A bite on a street, sidewalk, or trail, or in a park, happens in a public place, so the dog bite statute generally applies, while a knockdown by a loose dog is usually a negligence claim. Off-leash play is set apart in the city’s Central Bark Dog Park, so a bite or a fight there raises its own questions about who was in control of each dog and what the park’s rules required.' },
      { title: 'Homes, apartments, and common areas', body: 'A person is lawfully on private property when invited, expressly or by implication, or when there to perform a duty imposed by law, so a guest, delivery driver, or mail carrier bitten at an owner’s home or apartment is covered. In a rental community, the lease, the landlord’s knowledge of the dog, the pet registration, and any earlier complaints are reviewed along with the owner’s insurance.' },
      { title: 'Insurance coverage', body: 'Dog bite claims are often paid by the owner’s homeowner’s or renter’s insurance, but limits and exclusions vary, and an insurer may dispute coverage because of the dog, the household, or the property. The firm reads the actual policy, and any landlord’s coverage, before accepting an insurer’s position.' },
      { title: 'Reports, records, and deadlines', body: 'Irvine runs its own animal services through its police department and is not on OC Animal Care’s list of cities served. The city says Irvine Animal Services investigates bite reports and starts quarantines, and licensing records at the Irvine Animal Care Center, 6443 Oak Canyon, can help identify an owner. Most injury lawsuits are due in two years (Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1). A claim involving a police dog or other public property generally needs a written claim within six months (Government Code § 911.2), and claims against the City of Irvine go to its City Clerk. Unlimited civil cases are heard at the Central Justice Center, 700 Civic Center Drive West, Santa Ana.' },
    ],
    matters: [
      'Bites to the face, hands, and arms that leave scars',
      'Children bitten at a neighbor’s home, a party, or a neighborhood park',
      'Bites in apartment communities, courtyards, and other common areas, including claims involving landlords',
      'Delivery drivers, mail carriers, and other workers bitten on the job',
      'Knockdowns and falls caused by a loose or jumping dog on a trail or sidewalk',
      'Bites at or near an off-leash dog park',
    ],
    evidence: [
      'The Irvine Animal Services report number and any quarantine records',
      'The owner’s name and address, and their homeowner’s or renter’s insurance information',
      'For a rental, the landlord or property manager, the pet registration, and any earlier complaints about the dog',
      'Photos of the wounds as they heal, and of the gate, fence, or leash involved',
      'Torn clothing, kept as it is',
      'Witness names and any doorbell, phone, or building camera video, which can be overwritten quickly',
      'Medical records from the first visit forward, including wound checks, referrals, and counseling',
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: 'Is a dog owner in Irvine responsible if the dog never bit anyone before?', answer: 'Generally, yes. Under Civil Code § 3342, a dog’s owner is liable for a bite in a public place or while the injured person was lawfully on private property, including the owner’s home, regardless of the dog’s past behavior or the owner’s knowledge. Exceptions include a person who was not lawfully on the property and some police and military dog bites.' },
      { question: 'Who takes dog bite reports in Irvine?', answer: 'Irvine runs its own animal services instead of using the county’s. The Animal Services unit of the Irvine Police Department investigates bite reports and starts quarantines, and the city lists how to reach it on its Animal Services page. In an emergency, call 911. Ask for the report number, because it helps identify the dog, the owner, and the vaccination record.' },
      { question: 'What if a dog knocked me down on a trail or sidewalk but did not bite me?', answer: 'You may still have a claim, but not under the dog bite statute, which covers bites. A knockdown claim is usually based on negligence, such as letting a dog run loose, and the city’s leash rules and where the dog was allowed off leash are part of that review.' },
      { question: 'Can my apartment community be responsible for a neighbor’s dog?', answer: 'Sometimes the facts support a claim involving a landlord or property manager, but the dog’s owner is usually the first person responsible. The firm looks at what the landlord knew about the dog, earlier complaints, pet registrations, and emails to the leasing office, so keep copies.' },
      { question: 'Who pays for a dog bite injury?', answer: 'Often the owner’s homeowner’s or renter’s insurance. Limits and exclusions vary, some policies exclude certain dogs, and a landlord’s coverage may also apply. The firm reviews the actual policy before accepting an insurer’s position on coverage.' },
      { question: 'How long do I have to file a dog bite claim in California?', answer: 'Under Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1, most injury lawsuits must be filed within two years of the bite. If a public entity is involved, such as a police dog or a city park employee, a written government claim is generally due within six months under Government Code § 911.2. Report the bite to Irvine Animal Services right away, and confirm your deadlines early, because exceptions exist.' },
    ],
    featuredResults: ['Woman suffers wrist nerve damage from dog bite', 'Woman suffers dog bite injuries', 'Young man suffers dog bite injuries'],
