    faqs: [
      { question: 'Should I talk to an Orange brain injury lawyer if my CT scan was normal?', answer: 'It can still be worth a conversation. The CDC says a brain scan is not needed to spot a mild TBI or concussion, though one may be used when there is a risk of bleeding on the brain. A normal scan does not, by itself, rule out a concussion. Keep following up with your provider and write down symptoms that continue. A free consultation can help you decide whether a claim makes sense.' },
      { question: 'What if my symptoms started days after the crash or fall?', answer: 'That can happen. The CDC says some concussion symptoms appear right away, while others may not appear for hours or days after the injury. Get evaluated as soon as you notice them, tell the provider when they started, and keep a dated list. Insurers often question gaps in treatment, so prompt evaluation and steady follow-up help both your health and your claim.' },
      { question: 'My child was hurt at an Orange public school. How long do we have?', answer: 'Usually six months. A claim against a public school district, such as Orange Unified, generally must be presented in writing within six months under Government Code § 911.2. The rule that extends deadlines for minors, Code of Civil Procedure § 352, does not apply to these claims. If the six months pass, a late-claim application is possible, but generally no later than one year after the injury (§ 911.4).' },
      { question: 'What does California law require after a suspected concussion in youth sports?', answer: 'School athletic programs (Education Code § 49475) and youth sports organizations (Health and Safety Code § 124235) must remove an athlete suspected of a concussion or head injury for the rest of the day. The athlete cannot return until a licensed health care provider gives written clearance. A diagnosed concussion or head injury also requires a return-to-play protocol of at least seven days.' },
      { question: 'How can family members help prove a brain injury?', answer: 'People with a brain injury may not notice their own changes. The CDC says a person may not recognize or admit having problems, and that problems may be overlooked by family members or even providers. Family, teachers, coaches, and coworkers can describe how memory, mood, sleep, or schoolwork changed. Notes with dates, written close to the time, are especially helpful.' },
      { question: 'Does the six-month claim rule apply if the injury involved a public hospital or a city program?', answer: 'Generally, yes. California treats cities, school districts, and the Regents of the University of California as public entities (Government Code § 811.2), and a written claim is generally due within six months under § 911.2. That includes a city-run sports program or a city park. Claims against private businesses, private schools such as Chapman University, and private leagues generally do not need that step.' },
    ],
