    evidence: [
      'The Orange Police or CHP collision report, dispatch records, and witness contacts',
      'Scene photos, dashcam and surveillance video, and vehicle event data',
      'Emergency room, imaging, and follow-up records from the first visit forward',
      'Your own policy’s declarations page, especially the uninsured and underinsured motorist limits',
      'Repair estimates, rental receipts, and wage-loss records',
      'A copy of the DMV SR-1 report',
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: 'Should I hire an Orange car accident lawyer if the insurer already accepted fault?', answer: 'Accepting fault settles who caused the crash, not what the claim is worth. Insurers still dispute diagnoses, whether treatment was related, future care, and lost earnings, and a signed release usually ends the claim for good. A free consultation can help you decide whether an offer accounts for everything. Some people with minor, property-only claims handle them without a lawyer.' },
      { question: 'Who writes the police report for a crash in Orange, and how do I get a copy?', answer: 'The Orange Police Department’s Traffic Bureau handles collisions on city streets, and the California Highway Patrol handles the freeways. OPD’s Records Bureau releases collision reports to people who were injured or had property damaged, at its counter on North Batavia Street or through an online request. CHP reports are requested through the CHP Crash Portal or a CHP 190 form.' },
      { question: 'What is the Orange Crush, and who investigates crashes there?', answer: 'The Orange Crush is the interchange where the 5, the 22, and the 57 meet. The CHP says an average of 540,000 vehicles a day travel through it, and the CHP’s Santa Ana Area covers it. Get the report number at the scene if you can, and note the direction of travel and the nearest exit or connector ramp.' },
      { question: 'How long do I have to bring a car accident claim in California?', answer: 'Under Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1, most injury lawsuits must be filed within two years of the crash. If a public entity is involved, such as a City of Orange vehicle, a school district, or a public bus, a written government claim is generally due within six months under Government Code § 911.2. The DMV SR-1 report is due within 10 days. Exceptions exist, so confirm the dates early.' },
      { question: 'What if the driver who hit me had no insurance or drove away?', answer: 'If your policy includes uninsured motorist coverage, it may pay for your injuries. For a hit-and-run, Insurance Code § 11580.2 generally requires physical contact, a police report within 24 hours, and a sworn statement to your insurer within 30 days. Underinsured motorist coverage may help when the other driver’s limits are too low. California’s minimum is $30,000 for injury to one person, or $50,000 for a policy issued or renewed from January 1, 2035.' },
      { question: 'Can I still recover if I was partly at fault?', answer: 'Yes. California follows pure comparative fault: your damages are reduced by your percentage of fault, not eliminated (CACI No. 405). A separate rule, Civil Code § 3333.4 (Proposition 213), generally bars pain-and-suffering damages for an injured person who drove uninsured or was convicted of DUI for the crash, even if the other driver was at fault.' },
    ],
