    key: 'orange-dog-bites',
    path: '/orange-dog-bite-lawyer',
    shortTitle: 'Dog Bites',
    title: 'Orange, CA Dog Bite Lawyer',
    eyebrow: 'Dog bites in the City of Orange, California',
    description: 'If a dog bit you in the City of Orange, California law generally makes the owner responsible for a bite in a public place or while lawfully on private property, even if the dog never bit anyone before. Kyle Scott Law represents people bitten or knocked down by dogs on Old Towne sidewalks, in city parks, and in Orange neighborhoods and apartment communities.',
    metaDescription: 'Orange, CA dog bite lawyer for bites on Old Towne sidewalks, in city parks and in apartments, including people bitten protecting a pet. Free consultation.',
    icon: 'dog',
    introTitle: 'An Orange dog bite claim turns on where it happened and who pays.',
    intro: [
      'A dog’s owner is generally liable for a bite in a public place or while the injured person was lawfully on private property, including the owner’s own home, regardless of the dog’s history or the owner’s knowledge of it (Civil Code § 3342). Get medical care first, even for a bite that looks small: bites can become infected, and the medical record is also the first record of the injury. Then come the legal questions: who owned or was handling the dog, where the bite happened, and which insurance covers the owner. Scarring and nerve damage may not be clear for weeks, so an early settlement can leave out future care.',
      'Kyle Scott has practiced law since 1991 and has run his own firm since 2003. He identifies the owner and any landlord, obtains the bite report, and handles the insurance claim and any lawsuit. If you are looking for a dog bite attorney in Orange, your first conversation is with Kyle Scott himself.',
    ],
    keyPoints: [
      { title: 'Sidewalks, Old Towne, and city parks', body: 'A bite on an Old Towne sidewalk, in a city park, or on a neighborhood street happens in a public place, so the owner is generally liable without proof of carelessness. City law requires a dog on any public street, sidewalk, or park to be on a secure leash no longer than six feet (Orange Municipal Code § 12.48.056). The city says dogs in its parks, such as Hart Park and Grijalva Park, must stay leashed and with their owner, except at the Orange Dog Park at Yorba Park, 190 S. Yorba St. There, owners must take reasonable precautions to keep their dogs from biting and must remove a dog that bites.' },
      { title: 'Homes and apartment communities', body: 'A person is lawfully on private property when invited, expressly or by implication, or when there to perform a duty imposed by law, so a bite in an owner’s yard or apartment is covered. Guests, mail carriers, and others in that position are not trespassers. When the dog lives in a rental, the lease, the landlord’s knowledge of the dog, and any earlier complaints are reviewed along with the owner’s insurance.' },
      { title: 'Insurance coverage', body: 'The owner’s homeowner’s or renter’s insurance is often where a dog bite claim is paid from, but limits and exclusions vary, and an insurer may dispute coverage because of the dog, the household, or the property. The firm reads the actual policy and looks for any other coverage before accepting an insurer’s position.' },
      { title: 'Reports, quarantine, and deadlines', body: 'OC Animal Care lists Orange among the cities where it provides field services, and County Code § 4-1-62 requires anyone who knows of a bite to report it immediately. Orange adopts the county’s animal-control ordinances (Orange Municipal Code § 6.04.010). OC Animal Care quarantines a dog that bites a person for 10 days, at the owner’s home or at its center in Tustin, as a rabies precaution and not as a finding of fault. Most injury lawsuits are due in two years (Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1), and a claim involving a police dog or other public property generally needs a written claim within six months (Government Code § 911.2). Unlimited civil cases are heard at the Central Justice Center in Santa Ana; the Lamoreaux Justice Center in Orange hears juvenile and family cases, not injury lawsuits.' },
    ],
