    evidence: [
      'Texts, emails, chat and social media messages, and voicemails',
      'Notes of what happened, with dates, places, and who was there',
      'Complaints to HR, a manager, a school, or a landlord, and any responses',
      'Names of witnesses and of others who were treated the same way',
      'Schedules, pay records, and reviews from before and after a complaint',
      'Medical and counseling records, if and when you choose to share them',
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: 'Do I need a lawyer to file with the Civil Rights Department?', answer: 'No. You can submit an intake form to the Civil Rights Department yourself, online or by phone, within three years of the harassment. A lawyer can help you decide whether to request an investigation or an immediate right-to-sue notice, identify every responsible party, and meet the one-year lawsuit deadline that follows the notice. A consultation with Kyle Scott Law is free and confidential.' },
      { question: 'Can one incident count as sexual harassment in California?', answer: 'It can. Government Code § 12923 says a single incident of harassing conduct can be enough to create a triable issue of a hostile work environment, depending on the totality of the circumstances. The harassment need not be motivated by sexual desire, and you do not have to show lost pay or a missed promotion. Unwanted touching of an intimate part can also support a separate sexual battery claim under Civil Code § 1708.5.' },
      { question: 'I work for the County, a school district, or the City of Santa Ana. Is my claim different?', answer: 'Parts of it can be. The harassment rules apply to public employers, and those claims start with the Civil Rights Department. But other claims against a public entity, such as a sexual battery claim based on an employee’s conduct, generally require a written government claim within six months under Government Code § 911.2. Federal employees, including postal workers, generally must contact an agency EEO counselor within 45 days.' },
      { question: 'What if a doctor, landlord, or teacher harassed me?', answer: 'Civil Code § 51.9 covers sexual harassment in business, service, and professional relationships, including with physicians, psychotherapists, attorneys, landlords, property managers, teachers, and elected officials. The conduct must be unwelcome and pervasive or severe, and it must cause harm, such as economic loss or emotional distress. A complaint about this kind of harassment can also be filed with the Civil Rights Department within three years.' },
      { question: 'Will my name become public?', answer: 'The consultation is confidential. If a lawsuit is filed, a court may allow you to proceed under a pseudonym. Under Code of Civil Procedure § 1001, a settlement generally cannot bar disclosure of the facts of a sexual harassment or sexual assault claim filed in court or with an agency, but you can ask for a provision that shields your identity. Exceptions apply when a government agency or public official is a party.' },
      { question: 'Is a civil case separate from a criminal case?', answer: 'Yes. A criminal case is brought by the government and focuses on punishment. A civil case is brought by the survivor and seeks compensation and accountability, sometimes from an employer or institution as well as the person who caused the harm. The two can move forward at the same time. You can report to the Santa Ana Police Department or another agency and still speak with a lawyer.' },
    ],
